# Transport Management Database Roadmap

This document outlines the entire structure of the MySQL database used in the system, detailing the Entity Relationship (ER) routing, precise table columns, datatypes, and constraints (Nullable vs. Required fields).

## 1. Entity Relationship (ER) Diagram
*(The following diagram shows the logical relationships. Note: In this project's architecture, relations are currently defined using string-based implicit linking such as `vehicle_number` or `driver_name` instead of strict SQL Foreign Keys).*

```mermaid
erDiagram
    DRIVER ||--o{ VEHICLE : "assigned to"
    VEHICLE ||--o{ TRIP : "performs"
    DRIVER ||--o{ TRIP : "drives"
    VEHICLE ||--o{ FUEL : "consumes"
    VEHICLE ||--o{ TOLL : "pays"
    VEHICLE ||--o{ LRBILTY : "carries"
    DRIVER ||--o{ LRBILTY : "drives for"
    VEHICLE ||--o{ TRACKING : "monitored by"
    VENDOR ||--o{ INVENTORY : "supplies"

    VEHICLE {
        int vehicle_id PK
        string vehicle_number
        string driver_name FK "Implicit"
    }
    DRIVER {
        int driver_id PK
        string name
    }
    TRIP {
        int trip_id PK
        string vehicle_id FK
        string driver_name FK
    }
    FUEL {
        int fuel_id PK
        string vehicle FK
    }
    TOLL {
        int toll_id PK
        string vehicle FK
    }
    LRBILTY {
        int lr_id PK
        string eway FK
    }
    VENDOR {
        int vendor_id PK
        string name
    }
    INVENTORY {
        int item_id PK
        string vendor FK
    }
```

